-    "content": "# [Guide] Multi-Card Authentication Studio and Identity Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nEnterprise identity management demands both flexibility and strict security. Rather than forcing organizations into a single identity silo, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements a **Multi-Card Authentication Studio**. Administrators can configure, reorder, test, and enable multiple independent authentication providers simultaneously.\n\nSupported authentication card types:\n- **Local Admin:** Built-in argon2id hashed credentials for platform administrators.\n- **OAuth2 / OpenID Connect (OIDC):** Enterprise SSO supporting Okta, Google Workspace, GitHub, GitLab, Keycloak, and Authentik.\n- **LDAPS / StartTLS:** Active Directory and OpenLDAP directory integration with TLS certificate pinning.\n- **WebAuthn / Passkeys:** Hardware security keys (FIDO2/YubiKey) and platform biometrics (Touch ID, Windows Hello) with Relying Party display string `rp.name = \"\ud83d\udc4dkanban\"`.\n- **TOTP Authenticator:** Time-based one-time password second-factor enforcement.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Login[\"/login Surface (Multi-Card Container)\"] --> Cards[\"Ordered Cards by sort_rank\"]\n    Cards --> C1[\"Local Admin<br/>argon2id hash\"]\n    Cards --> C2[\"OAuth2 / OIDC<br/>Enterprise SSO & Claims\"]\n    Cards --> C3[\"LDAPS / StartTLS<br/>Pinned TLS certs\"]\n    Cards --> C4[\"WebAuthn Passkeys<br/>FIDO2 / TouchID / YubiKey\"]\n\n    C1 --> Verify[\"Session Token Minting\"]\n    C2 --> Verify\n    C3 --> Verify\n    C4 --> Verify\n\n    Verify --> MFA{\"MFA Enforced?\"}\n    MFA -->|Yes| TOTP[\"TOTP Second Factor / Recovery Codes\"]\n    MFA -->|No| Session[\"AAL1 Session Minted\"]\n    TOTP --> AAL2[\"AAL2 Session Minted (Admin-Capable)\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## First-Boot Setup Wizard (`/setup`)\n\nOn initial installation, \ud83d\udc4dkanban boots into a secure provisioning state. Until the platform is initialized, all routes redirect to `/setup`.\n\n![First-Boot Setup Wizard](images/01-first-boot-setup.png)\n\n### Bootstrap Security Invariants\n\n1. **Single-Use Bootstrap Token (`kb_setup_*`):** When the container starts for the first time, a cryptographically secure token prefixed with `kb_setup_` is generated and written exclusively to container logs or stdout. Initial account creation requires providing this token.\n2. **Pre-Session CSRF Protection:** First-boot requests establish a strict double-submit cookie handshake using `__Host-kb_csrf` (over HTTPS) and `kb_csrf` (fallback) headers to prevent cross-site onboarding attacks.\n3. **Atomic Root Workspace Creation:** The wizard provisions the initial Server Administrator, creates the default workspace and project, and immediately invalidates the bootstrap token.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Card Login Experience (`/login`)\n\nThe login screen presents enabled authentication methods as clean, discrete cards sorted in the exact sequence determined by the administrator.\n\n![Multi-Card Login Screen](images/02-multi-card-login.png)\n\n### Key Features of Multi-Card Login\n\n- **Sort Order Control:** Providers are displayed according to their configured `sort_rank`, allowing primary enterprise SSO cards to take prominent visual priority.\n- **Zero Implicit Email Auto-Merge Invariant:** A critical security flaw in many SaaS systems is the automatic linking of accounts based solely on matching email addresses. In \ud83d\udc4dkanban, auto-merging is strictly forbidden. An identity claim from an external provider will only bind to an existing account if the user explicitly authorizes the link through verified secondary credentials.\n- **Passkey and Biometric Priority:** Users with registered WebAuthn credentials can authenticate with a single touch, without entering usernames or passwords.\n\n---\n\n## Admin Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`)\n\nServer Administrators configure identity providers through the visual Auth Studio at `/admin/auth`. Access to this studio requires Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) credentials.\n\n![Admin Authentication Studio](images/03-admin-auth-studio.png)\n\n### Studio Controls and Capabilities\n\n- **Enable / Disable Toggles:** Instantly activate or suspend identity providers without losing configuration settings.\n- **Drag-and-Drop Reordering:** Change the display order of cards on the `/login` screen in real time.\n- **Write-Only Secrets:** Client secrets, LDAP bind passwords, and private keys are write-only. Once saved, they cannot be read back by client browsers or administrative APIs.\n- **Live Handshake Testing:** The `Test Handshake` button validates endpoint connectivity, TLS certificate validation, and credential binding without modifying live user sessions.\n\n### Envelope Encryption (`MASTER_KEK`)\n\nAll sensitive authentication secrets stored in the database are encrypted at rest using envelope encryption:\n- Secrets are encrypted using an AES-256-GCM data encryption key (DEK).\n- The DEK is encrypted using the platform Master Key Encryption Key (`MASTER_KEK`), supplied via environment variable or hardware security module (HSM).\n\n### The `LOCKOUT_PREVENTED` Invariant\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Server Administrator\n    participant UI as Auth Studio (/admin/auth)\n    participant API as /api/v1/admin/auth/cards\n    participant DB as PostgreSQL\n\n    Admin->>UI: Toggle 'Local Admin' card to Disabled\n    UI->>API: PATCH /cards/{id} (is_active: false)\n    API->>DB: Query COUNT(active admin cards) excluding this card\n    alt Remaining Active Admin Cards == 0\n        DB-->>API: 0 Active Admin Paths Remain!\n        API-->>UI: 409 Conflict (LOCKOUT_PREVENTED)\n        UI-->>Admin: Show Error Modal: Cannot disable sole remaining admin card\n    else Remaining Active Admin Cards >= 1\n        DB-->>API: Active admin paths exist (e.g. OIDC configured)\n        API->>DB: UPDATE auth_cards SET is_active = false\n        DB-->>API: 200 OK\n        API-->>UI: Card disabled successfully\n    end\n```\n\nTo eliminate administrative lockouts:\n- The system continuously evaluates the active admin authentication paths.\n- If an administrator attempts to disable or delete the sole remaining active admin card (e.g., disabling Local Admin before configuring working SSO), the mutation is rejected with error code `LOCKOUT_PREVENTED`.\n- At least one verified administrative authentication path must remain active at all times.\n\n### Multi-Factor Authentication and Recovery Codes\n\nDuring administrator provisioning and MFA enrollment, high-entropy single-use recovery codes are minted, cryptographically hashed, and presented to the operator:\n\n![MFA Provisioning and Recovery Codes](images/34-mfa-recovery-codes.png)\n"
+    "content": "# [Guide] Multi-Card Authentication Studio and Identity Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nEnterprise identity management demands both flexibility and strict security. Rather than forcing organizations into a single identity silo, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements a **Multi-Card Authentication Studio**. Administrators can configure, reorder, test, and enable multiple independent authentication providers simultaneously.\n\nSupported authentication card types:\n- **Local Admin:** Built-in argon2id hashed credentials for platform administrators.\n- **OAuth2 / OpenID Connect (OIDC):** Enterprise SSO supporting Okta, Google Workspace, GitHub, GitLab, Keycloak, and Authentik.\n- **LDAPS / StartTLS:** Active Directory and OpenLDAP directory integration with TLS certificate pinning and nested group sync.\n- **WebAuthn / Passkeys:** Hardware security keys (FIDO2/YubiKey) and platform biometrics (Touch ID, Windows Hello) with Relying Party display string `rp.name = \"\ud83d\udc4dkanban\"`.\n- **TOTP Authenticator:** Time-based one-time password second-factor enforcement.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Login[\"/login Surface (Multi-Card Container)\"] --> Cards[\"Ordered Cards by sort_rank\"]\n    Cards --> C1[\"Local Admin - argon2id hash\"]\n    Cards --> C2[\"OAuth2 / OIDC - Enterprise SSO and Claims\"]\n    Cards --> C3[\"LDAPS / StartTLS - Pinned TLS certs\"]\n    Cards --> C4[\"WebAuthn Passkeys - FIDO2 / TouchID / YubiKey\"]\n\n    C1 --> Verify[\"Session Token Minting\"]\n    C2 --> Verify\n    C3 --> Verify\n    C4 --> Verify\n\n    Verify --> MFA{\"MFA Enforced?\"}\n    MFA -->|Yes| TOTP[\"TOTP Second Factor / Recovery Codes\"]\n    MFA -->|No| Session[\"AAL1 Session Minted\"]\n    TOTP --> AAL2[\"AAL2 Session Minted (Admin-Capable)\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## First-Boot Setup Wizard\n\nOn initial installation, \ud83d\udc4dkanban boots into a secure provisioning state. Until the platform is initialized, all routes redirect to `/setup`.\n\n![First-Boot Setup Wizard](images/01-first-boot-setup.png)\n\n### Bootstrap Security Invariants\n\n1. **Single-Use Bootstrap Token** (`kb_setup_*`): When the container starts for the first time, a cryptographically secure token prefixed with `kb_setup_` is generated and written exclusively to container logs or stdout. Initial account creation requires providing this token.\n2. **Pre-Session CSRF Protection:** First-boot requests establish a strict double-submit cookie handshake using `__Host-kb_csrf` (over HTTPS) and `kb_csrf` (fallback) headers to prevent cross-site onboarding attacks.\n3. **Atomic Root Workspace Creation:** The wizard provisions the initial Server Administrator, creates the default workspace and project, and immediately invalidates the bootstrap token.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Card Login Experience\n\nThe login screen presents enabled authentication methods as clean, discrete cards sorted in the exact sequence determined by the administrator.\n\n![Multi-Card Login Screen - Desktop Light](images/slice1-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Key Features of Multi-Card Login\n\n- **Sort Order Control:** Providers are displayed according to their configured `sort_rank`, allowing primary enterprise SSO cards to take prominent visual priority while preserving fallback admin access.\n- **Zero Implicit Email Auto-Merge Invariant:** A critical security flaw in many SaaS systems is the automatic linking of accounts based solely on matching email addresses. In \ud83d\udc4dkanban, auto-merging is strictly forbidden. An identity claim from an external provider will only bind to an existing account if the user explicitly authorizes the link through verified secondary credentials.\n- **Passkey and Biometric Priority:** Users with registered WebAuthn credentials can authenticate with a single touch, without entering usernames or passwords.\n\n---\n\n## Admin Authentication Studio\n\nServer Administrators configure identity providers through the visual Auth Studio at `/admin/auth`. Access to this studio requires Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) credentials.\n\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice1-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice1-desktop-1024x768.png)\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice1-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice1-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Studio Controls and Capabilities\n\n- **Enable / Disable Toggles:** Instantly activate or suspend identity providers without losing configuration settings.\n- **Drag-and-Drop Reordering:** Change the display order of cards on the `/login` screen in real time.\n- **Write-Only Secrets:** Client secrets, LDAP bind passwords, and private keys are write-only. Once saved, they cannot be read back by client browsers or administrative APIs.\n- **Live Handshake Testing:** The `Test Handshake` button validates endpoint connectivity, TLS certificate validation, and credential binding without modifying live user sessions.\n  - Draft Cards: `POST /api/v1/admin/auth-cards/test-handshake` validates unsaved drawer parameters before committing to PostgreSQL.\n  - Saved Cards: `POST /api/v1/admin/auth-cards/{card_ref}/test-handshake` tests an active or disabled card against live network endpoints.\n\n### Envelope Encryption (`MASTER_KEK`)\n\nAll sensitive authentication secrets stored in the database are encrypted at rest using envelope encryption:\n- Secrets are encrypted using an AES-256-GCM data encryption key (DEK).\n- The DEK is encrypted using the platform Master Key Encryption Key (`MASTER_KEK`), supplied via environment variable or hardware security module (HSM).\n\n---\n\n## Lockout Prevention\n\nTo prevent catastrophic platform lockouts where administrators lose all access:\n- The system continuously evaluates active administrative authentication pathways before permitting any card modification or deletion.\n- If an administrator attempts to disable or delete the sole remaining active admin card (e.g., disabling Local Admin before configuring working SSO), the mutation is rejected with RFC 7807 problem code `409 Conflict` (`LOCKOUT_PREVENTED`).\n- Disabling or deleting the local admin card strictly requires that an external provider card has an active administrator account mapping AND has completed a successful handshake probe within the last 15 minutes (`handshake_probe_15m` layer, `HANDSHAKE_PROBE_MAX_AGE_SECONDS = 900`).\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Server Administrator\n    participant UI as Auth Studio (/admin/auth)\n    participant API as /api/v1/admin/auth-cards\n    participant DB as PostgreSQL (kb_auth)\n\n    Admin->>UI: Toggle Local Admin card to Disabled\n    UI->>API: PATCH /api/v1/admin/auth-cards/{id} (is_enabled: false)\n    API->>DB: Query active admin cards excluding target card\n    alt Remaining Active Admin Cards == 0\n        DB-->>API: 0 Active Admin Paths Remain\n        API-->>UI: 409 Conflict (LOCKOUT_PREVENTED)\n        UI-->>Admin: Show Error Modal: Cannot disable sole remaining admin card\n    else External Card Active but Probe > 15m Old\n        API-->>UI: 409 Conflict (handshake_probe_15m required)\n        UI-->>Admin: Error: Test Handshake probe required within last 15 minutes\n    else Remaining Active Admin Card Verified within 15m\n        API->>DB: UPDATE auth_provider_cards SET is_enabled = false\n        DB-->>API: 200 OK\n        API-->>UI: Card disabled successfully\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## AAL2 Step-Up\n\nAll administrative surfaces and sensitive mutations require Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) step-up authentication (`Depends(require_admin_aal2)`).\n\n![WebSudo Replay Active](images/slice1-websudo-replay-active.png)\n\n### WebSudo Replay Architecture\n\nWhen an administrator attempts a sensitive action (such as saving a newly configured LDAP card, modifying role bindings, or minting API tokens) after their session has fallen back to AAL1:\n1. The backend responds with HTTP 403 `AAL2_REQUIRED`.\n2. The frontend intercepts the response, captures the pending mutation in an in-memory queue, and opens the WebSudo elevation modal over the dirty editor drawer.\n3. The administrator enters their password, TOTP code, or touches their passkey to elevate to AAL2.\n4. Upon successful elevation, the pending mutation is automatically replayed with the newly elevated session credentials. Dirty form inputs are preserved without data loss.\n5. The elevated AAL2 session remains active for a sliding window of 900 seconds (15 minutes). Explicit step-down is available via `POST /api/v1/auth/aal2/drop`.\n\n---\n\n## MFA Enrollment\n\nMulti-factor authentication provides an essential layer of security for all user tiers and is strictly required for Server Administrators.\n\n![TOTP Recovery Codes Generation](images/slice1-totp-recovery-codes.png)\n![MFA Provisioning and Recovery Codes](images/34-mfa-recovery-codes.png)\n\n### Supported MFA Mechanisms\n\n- **TOTP (Time-Based One-Time Password):** Standard RFC 6238 TOTP using compatible mobile authenticator applications (Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, 1Password, Bitwarden).\n  - Enrollment endpoint: `POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/totp/enroll` generates base32 secret and QR code URI.\n  - Confirmation endpoint: `POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/totp/confirm` validates the first 6-digit code to activate MFA on the account.\n  - Verification endpoint: `POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/verify` validates subsequent codes during login and WebSudo step-up.\n- **Hardware Security Keys and WebAuthn Passkeys:** Direct FIDO2 browser authentication.\n  - Registration: Uses standard browser navigator.credentials.create with Relying Party display name `rp.name = \"\ud83d\udc4dkanban\"` and ASCII FQDN `rp.id`.\n- **Break-Glass Backup Recovery Codes:** High-entropy single-use recovery codes generated during MFA enrollment.\n  - Stored in PostgreSQL using secure one-way salted hashes.\n  - Regenerated via `POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/recovery/regenerate`.\n  - Once a code is used, it is permanently consumed and cannot be reused.\n\n---\n\n## Active Directory and LDAPS Integration\n\nThe LDAP provider card connects \ud83d\udc4dkanban directly to Microsoft Active Directory or OpenLDAP directory trees over secure encrypted channels.\n\n![LDAP Test Draft Handshake Success](images/slice1-ldap-test-draft-success.png)\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Connecting Active Directory\n\n1. **Navigate to Auth Studio:** Open `/admin/auth` and click **Add Authentication Card**, then select **LDAP / Active Directory**.\n2. **Configure Server Connection:** Enter the Domain Controller FQDN in **LDAP Host** (e.g., `dc1.corp.example.com`), select port `636` for LDAPS or `389` for StartTLS, choose **Security Mode**, and optionally supply a custom PEM **CA Certificate** for private enterprise PKI. Plaintext LDAP is strictly rejected.\n3. **Configure Service Account Bind:** Enter the read-only service account distinguished name in **Bind DN** (e.g., `CN=KanbanService,OU=ServiceAccounts,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com`) and provide the **Bind Password** (stored encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM envelope encryption).\n4. **Define User Search Scope:** Specify **User Base DN** (e.g., `OU=Users,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com`), enter **User Search Filter** syntax matching `sAMAccountName` or `userPrincipalName` (e.g., `(&(objectCategory=person)(sAMAccountName={username}))`), and configure **Email Attribute** (`mail`) and **Display Name Attribute** (`displayName`).\n5. **Configure Active Directory Nested Group Sync:** Specify **Group Base DN** (e.g., `OU=SecurityGroups,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com`), enter **Group Search Filter** utilizing the recursive AD `memberOf` rule `(&(objectCategory=group)(member:1.2.840.113556.1.4.1941:={user_dn}))`, and configure group mappings to \ud83d\udc4dkanban workspace and project roles (`WORKSPACE_ADMIN`, `PROJECT_ADMIN`, `WORKSPACE_MEMBER`).\n6. **Execute Draft Handshake Probe:** Click **Test Handshake** before saving. The backend executes `POST /api/v1/admin/auth-cards/test-handshake` to verify TLS certificate negotiation and directory bind. Once green, click **Create Card**.\n\n---\n\n## OpenID Connect and Google OAuth SSO\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban supports OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity providers including Google Workspace, Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD), Okta, Keycloak, and Authentik.\n\n![OIDC Verified Claim Linked](images/slice1-oidc-verified-claim-linked.png)\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Configuring Google and Keycloak OIDC\n\n1. **Register OAuth Client:** In Google Cloud Console or Keycloak Admin Console, create an OAuth 2.0 Web Application client and set Authorized Redirect URI to `https://your-domain.example.com/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback` (e.g., `https://kanban.corp.example.com/api/v1/auth/oidc/google/callback`).\n2. **Open Auth Studio:** Navigate to `/admin/auth`, click **Add Authentication Card**, and select **OpenID Connect (OIDC)** or **Google Workspace**.\n3. **Configure Core Parameters:** Enter a lowercase ASCII identifier in **Card Slug** (e.g., `google` or `keycloak-corp`), user-facing label in **Display Name** (e.g., `Google Workspace SSO`), and discovery endpoint in **Issuer URL** (e.g., `https://accounts.google.com`).\n4. **Enter Credentials or Import JSON:** Input **Client ID** and **Client Secret** (AES-256-GCM encrypted), or click **Import Google OAuth JSON** in the drawer to upload downloaded client secret JSON directly.\n5. **Enforce Verified Claims and Domain Allowlist:** Specify requested scopes (`openid email profile`). The backend strictly enforces `email_verified: true` (rejecting unverified claims with HTTP 403 `EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED`). Configure corporate domains in **Allowed Email Domains** (e.g., `corp.example.com`).\n6. **Test Handshake and Enable:** Click **Test Handshake** to verify discovery document retrieval and endpoint reachability. Save the card and toggle **Enabled**.\n\n---\n\n## WebAuthn Passkeys and TOTP Enrollment\n\nWebAuthn Passkeys offer cryptographic, phishing-resistant authentication backed by hardware security chips (Apple Secure Enclave, Google Titan, YubiKey).\n\n![Passkey WebAuthn Registered Successfully](images/slice1-passkey-webauthn-registered.png)\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Enrolling Passkeys and TOTP\n\n1. **Open Personal Settings:** Navigate to `/settings/profile` and select the **Security** tab.\n2. **Enroll Hardware Passkey:** Click **Register Passkey / Security Key** and complete platform biometrics or FIDO2 YubiKey touch. Relying Party parameters (`rp.name = \"\ud83d\udc4dkanban\"`, strict ASCII FQDN `rp.id`) protect against phishing. Assign a recognizable label (e.g., `MacBook Pro Touch ID` or `Backup YubiKey 5C`).\n3. **Enroll Authenticator App (TOTP):** Click **Enroll Authenticator App**, scan the rendered QR code with your authenticator application or copy the base32 secret key, and enter the current 6-digit verification code to confirm setup.\n4. **Store Break-Glass Recovery Codes:** Upon confirming MFA, the platform displays 8 single-use backup recovery codes. Store these codes in an encrypted password manager or secure physical safe. If your hardware key or phone is lost, recovery codes allow emergency account access.\n5. **Emergency Account Recovery:** In the event that your primary and second factors are lost, enter a single-use recovery code at the MFA prompt to restore account access and immediately regenerate your recovery set.\n\n---\n\n## Password Security\n\nFor users authenticating via the Local Admin card or local credentials:\n- **Minimum Entropy Standards:** Passwords must be at least 12 characters in length (14 characters for Server Administrators).\n- **Cryptographic Hashing:** Passwords are never stored in cleartext. Credentials are hashed using state-of-the-art Argon2id with memory cost 65536 KiB, time cost 3 iterations, and parallelism 4.\n- **Break-Glass Emergency Access:** In the event that all external identity providers are unreachable, the local administrator card ensures immediate platform recovery.\n"
   },
